ntfs-macos patch 0007: flush a device node with fsync then DKIOCSYNCHRONIZE (macOS only)

Rationale (T-M3-21, ADR-022, ADR-014, docs/spikes/M3-sync.md): on macOS upstream ntfs_fsync() calls
fcntl(fd, F_FULLFSYNC) and, if that returns non-zero for any reason, silently falls back to
fsync(fd). Measured on macOS 27.2 (T-M3-10): F_FULLFSYNC fails with ENOTTY on every block and
character device node tried (/dev/diskN and /dev/rdiskN of a disk image, and of a VirtIO disk), so
on a device node the fallback is the normal path, fsync() on a device node does not ask the drive
to flush its write cache, and nothing reports it. ioctl(DKIOCSYNCHRONIZE) returned 0 on the same
nodes. ntfs-macos mounts device nodes, so without this patch no sync point of the worker (an
application's fsync, the unmount) flushes the drive cache.
Hunks (libntfs-3g/unix_io.c only):
  - on __APPLE__, #include <sys/disk.h> (DKIOCSYNCHRONIZE, dk_synchronize_t), before ntfs_fsync().
  - ntfs_fsync(), on __APPLE__ only: the descriptor type decides. fstat() says block or character
    device: fsync(fd), then ioctl(fd, DKIOCSYNCHRONIZE, &{offset 0, length 0, options 0}), which
    flushes the whole device; the function returns fsync()'s result. A failed ioctl is logged with
    ntfs_log_perror() and does not change the result (a drive that cannot be flushed must not turn
    a session that wrote its data into a failed one; ntfs-macos patch 0006 reports it as a
    "W flush" record). errno is fsync()'s when fsync() failed. Regular file (disk image, tests), or
    fstat() fails: the upstream code is unchanged, F_FULLFSYNC then the fsync() fallback.
Callers are unchanged: ntfs_device_unix_io_sync(), ntfs_device_unix_io_close() and, through
__ntfs_volume_release(), every read/write unmount reach the new code, and ntfs_device_sync() still
skips a device that is not dirty.
Scope: device layer, one function, __APPLE__ only. The Linux and other-OS build is the upstream
code (the new lines are inside #if defined(__APPLE__) || defined(__DARWIN__)); only line numbers
after the hunk move. Nothing touches allocation, runlists, MFT or index code. The patch applies
with and without patch 0004 (the faultio macros are on other lines).
Upstream status: not submitted. Sending is a human decision. Report text (for the ntfs-3g
project, https://github.com/tuxera/ntfs-3g/issues):
  Title: macOS: ntfs_fsync() never flushes the drive cache of a device node
  Body:  On macOS libntfs-3g/unix_io.c ntfs_fsync() calls fcntl(fd, F_FULLFSYNC) and falls back to
         fsync(fd) if that fails. F_FULLFSYNC is a file system operation: on a block or character
         device node (/dev/diskN, /dev/rdiskN) it fails with ENOTTY on macOS 27.2 (checked on a disk
         image attached with hdiutil and on a VirtIO disk). The fallback then runs at every sync
         point and the failure is silent, so ntfs-3g mounted from a device node never asks the
         drive to flush its write cache, at unmount or on fsync. On such a node the device-level
         call is ioctl(fd, DKIOCSYNCHRONIZE, &dk_synchronize_t) from <sys/disk.h>; it returned 0 on
         the same nodes. Proposed change: when fstat() says S_ISBLK or S_ISCHR, call fsync() then
         ioctl(DKIOCSYNCHRONIZE) with a zeroed dk_synchronize_t (whole device); keep F_FULLFSYNC
         for regular files. Return fsync()'s result and log a failed flush. Not tested on a
         physical drive. Patch attached (this file's diff).
Test: I-SYNC-01, I-SYNC-02 (tests/integration/sync/i_sync.py with syncspy.c): on a buffered
device node every sync point that reaches a dirty device, and the unmount, shows fsync then
DKIOCSYNCHRONIZE returning 0 and no F_FULLFSYNC; an image file still shows one F_FULLFSYNC.
Real drives: Environment C (H-USB-*), where the effect of the flush on a drive cache is checked.
The character-device branch (/dev/rdiskN) is covered only by the T-M3-10 spike (ioctl returns 0):
the raw node does not mount, so I-SYNC has no sync point on it.

diff -ruN a/libntfs-3g/unix_io.c b/libntfs-3g/unix_io.c
--- a/libntfs-3g/unix_io.c	2026-11-15 08:53:12.600348577 +0000
+++ b/libntfs-3g/unix_io.c	2026-11-15 08:53:35.028641480 +0000
@@ -105,6 +105,10 @@
 #	define O_EXCL 0
 #endif
 
+#if defined(__APPLE__) || defined(__DARWIN__)
+#include <sys/disk.h>
+#endif
+
 /**
  * fsync replacement which makes every effort to try to get the data down to
  * disk, using different means for different operating systems. Specifically,
@@ -120,6 +124,30 @@
 # ifndef F_FULLFSYNC
 #  error "Mac OS X: F_FULLFSYNC is not defined. Either you didn't include fcntl.h or you're using an older, unsupported version of Mac OS X (pre-10.3)."
 # endif
+	struct stat st;
+
+	/*
+	 * ntfs-macos patch 0007 (ADR-022): a block or character device
+	 * refuses F_FULLFSYNC with ENOTTY, so below it would only fall
+	 * through to a plain fsync() and never ask the drive to flush its
+	 * cache. Flush a device node with fsync() and then
+	 * ioctl(DKIOCSYNCHRONIZE), the device equivalent. The result is
+	 * fsync()'s; a failed flush is logged and does not change it.
+	 * Regular files keep the code below unchanged.
+	 */
+	if (!fstat(fildes, &st) && (S_ISBLK(st.st_mode) || S_ISCHR(st.st_mode))) {
+		dk_synchronize_t dk;
+		int err;
+
+		ret = fsync(fildes);
+		err = errno;
+		memset(&dk, 0, sizeof(dk));
+		if (ioctl(fildes, DKIOCSYNCHRONIZE, &dk))
+			ntfs_log_perror("DKIOCSYNCHRONIZE failed");
+		if (ret)
+			errno = err;
+		return ret;
+	}
 	/* 
 	 * Apple has disabled fsync() for internal disk drives in OS X.
 	 * To force a synchronization of disk contents, we use a Mac OS X
